    {
      "type": "breakdown",
      "heading": "Sentence breakdown",
      "sentence": {
        "line": {
          "gurmukhi": "ਮੈਂ ਰੋਟੀ ਖਾਂਦਾ ਹਾਂ।",
          "romanised": "Main roti khanda haan.",
          "english": "I eat bread. (male speaker)"
        }
      },
      "glosses": [
        { "gurmukhi": "ਮੈਂ", "romanised": "main", "meaning": "I", "grammarNote": "subject" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਰੋਟੀ", "romanised": "roti", "meaning": "bread", "grammarNote": "object — before the verb" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਖਾਂਦਾ", "romanised": "khanda", "meaning": "eat", "grammarNote": "habitual, masculine — the speaker is male" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਹਾਂ", "romanised": "haan", "meaning": "am", "grammarNote": "helper verb — completes the present tense" }
      ],
      "note": "The everyday present is a two-part verb: action word with a gender ending (khanda/khandi/khande) + a small helper (haan/hai/ho/han)."
    },
    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "Two shapes to learn",
      "body": "1. The infinitive — \"to do\": verb stem + ਣਾ/ਨਾ (na). jaana = to go, khaana = to eat, bolna = to speak. Use it in sentences like \"I want to learn Punjabi\" or \"kirpa karke bolna\" patterns later.\n\n2. The everyday present — \"I do\": subject + object + verb-with-ending + helper.\n\n   main roti khanda haan — I eat bread (male speaker)\n   main roti khandi haan — (female speaker)\n   tusi roti khande ho — you eat bread (polite)\n\nThe verb ending matches the subject's gender and politeness — bolda/boldi/bolde, not because the action is gendered, but because Punjabi verbs agree with who is doing them."
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "English hides agreement: \"I eat\", \"you eat\", \"she eats\" barely change. Punjabi verbs wear their subject openly — khand-a vs khand-i tells you the speaker is male or female before you even look up.\n\nIt feels like extra work, but it also means pronouns are often dropped: a Punjabi speaker might just say \"roti khandi haan\" and the verb still tells you who and what gender is eating. You are not memorising endings — you are hearing information English doesn't give you."
    },
